= Question 3

An earthquake in Vancouver had a magnitude of 6.3 on the Richter scale. An earthquake in Japan

had a magnitude of 8.9 on the Richter scale.

How many times more intense was the Japan earthquake than the Vancouver earthquake?

You may use the formula below:

$  M = log  lr(\( frac(A ,A _(0 ))\) )  $

where $M $ is the magnitude of the earthquake on the Richter scale

$A $ is the intensity of the earthquake

$A _(0 )$ is the intensity of a standard earthquake

Express your answer as a whole number.

== Solution

Method 1

Vancouver: substitute $M = 6.3 $

$   6.3  & = log  lr(\( frac(A ,A _(0 ))\) ) \  10 ^(6.3 ) & = frac(A ,A _(0 )) \  A  & = 10 ^(6.3 ) A _(0 )   $

$frac(1 ,2 )$ mark for exponential form

Japan: substitute $M = 8.9 $

$   8.9  & = log  lr(\( frac(A ,A _(0 ))\) ) \  10 ^(8.9 ) & = frac(A ,A _(0 )) \  A  & = 10 ^(8.9 ) A _(0 )   $

$frac(1 ,2 )$ mark for exponential form

To compare the two earthquakes divide their intensities.

$   frac(" the intensity of Japan "," the intensity of Vancouver ") & = frac(10 ^(8.9 ) A _(0 ),10 ^(6.3 ) A _(0 )) \  & = 398.107  \  & = 398    $

1 mark for comparison

2 marks

